WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:06.440
Bond gets a Bridesmaids-style make-over, with
a touch of Miss Congeniality thrown in for

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:10.540
good measure, in writer-director Paul Feig's
new movie, Spy.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:15.860
But don't go thinking that Spy is just a spoof
of Bond movies; there's no winking at the

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:21.320
camera here; instead the humour's played straight-faced,
and Spy works as a proper action-comedy.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:28.070
The movie kicks off with a Bond-esque pre-credits
sequence followed by its own spin on a 007-style

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.720
song and titles.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:35.590
Yet, in a don't-judge-a-book-by-its-cover
twist, the hero of Spy's story isn't a James

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:38.260
Bond, Jason Bourne, or Jack Bauer type.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:45.510
It's Susan Cooper, a deskbound analyst whose
entire CIA career so far has been spent in

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.120
a vermin-infested basement office.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:53.250
Of course, that changes and in true undercover
agent style, Spy takes us location hopping

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:58.120
around various European cities including Paris,
Rome, and Budapest.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:03.190
Melissa McCarthy really gets to shine in her
third film with Bridesmaids and The Heat director

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:04.320
Paul Feig.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.270
She does a great job in the lead, playing
what feels like a more multi-faceted character

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.270
than she has before.

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:15.950
The role of Susan Cooper gives McCarthy the
opportunity to bring to life a sweet-natured,

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:21.049
considerate and extremely competent character,
but also gives her the chance to explore a

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:26.479
side to that character that's bawdier, more
bolshie and, most importantly, more badass.

00:01:26.479 --> 00:01:30.770
If gross-out gags are your bag, then there's
definitely something in Spy for you.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:35.200
But, if like me, you could live without that
type of comedy, don't be put off as there's

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.130
much more to the movie than that.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:42.170
As we've seen before, McCarthy's at home with
both dialogue-driven comedy and more physical

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:43.950
and visual slapstick humour.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:48.420
For example, she has a lot of fun with her
various spy gadgets and aliases.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:53.439
But what really makes Spy stand out, and provides
some of the movie's biggest laugh-out-loud

00:01:53.439 --> 00:01:58.600
moments, is the superb support and interaction
McCarthy has with her co-stars.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.759
As McCarthy's in-the-field partner, Jude Law
gets to do a brilliant back-door audition

00:02:03.759 --> 00:02:07.350
for the role of James Bond, kind of in the
vein of Roger Moore.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:13.780
Law's super-spy Bradley Fine has all the right
moves, the charm and unrivalled levels of

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:15.250
self-absorption.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:20.840
Jason Statham may be best known for his tough
guy turns in movie franchises like The Expendables

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:26.310
and Transporter, but in Spy he gets the chance
to show his comedy chops by sending up his

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:32.400
usual action movie persona to hilarious effect,
and there's a fabulously knowing joke about

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.019
a particular Nic Cage movie!

00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:40.409
Statham's character, a hard-nut CIA agent
called Rick Ford, comes across as the kind

00:02:40.409 --> 00:02:45.579
of guy who's watched all of Statham's action
movies and confused them with reality.

00:02:45.579 --> 00:02:51.189
Yes, Ford's so tough that when he touches
his stubbled chin, you can actually hear a

00:02:51.189 --> 00:02:53.060
loud bristling noise!

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:58.819
However, his main skill lies in an unparalleled
ability to talk up his supposedly many and

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:02.049
varied utterly incredible exploits.

00:03:02.049 --> 00:03:07.150
His totally preposterous anecdotes are funny
enough in themselves – I particularly liked

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:12.629
the ones about the Cirque de Soleil and poisons
–, but what really makes them side-splittingly

00:03:12.629 --> 00:03:18.920
hysterical is the way he recounts them, completely
deadpan, with one outrageous detail or story

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.690
piled on top of another.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:26.249
Rose Byrne puts in a great performance as
the movie's super-snobby baddie Rayna, who

00:03:26.249 --> 00:03:31.579
never has a hair out of place or a make-up
malfunction and loves nothing more than looking

00:03:31.579 --> 00:03:34.019
down her nose at, well, everyone.

00:03:34.019 --> 00:03:37.560
I love West Wing star Allison Janney in pretty
much anything.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:43.010
Here, she plays McCarthy's CIA boss in Spy,
and her reaction after watching an old video

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:46.299
of McCarthy at a CIA training camp is priceless.

00:03:46.299 --> 00:03:53.280
As McCarthy's best CIA bud, Miranda Hart makes
a great, goofy, gawky addition to the cast.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.920
Even though his character seems to be a walking,
talking stereotype, Peter Serafinowicz is

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.221
very funny as an extremely handsy Italian
agent.

00:04:02.221 --> 00:04:07.840
Oh, and there's a couple of cameos in the
movie too – one of them is a lot more obvious

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.959
than the other!

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:16.430
On top of its good dose of comedy, Spy has
a number of decent and some very good action

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:21.150
scenes, including some hand-to-hand combat
that reminded me of Jason Bourne's fight with

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:27.220
hitman Desh in The Bourne Ultimatum, although
Spy's version is in a rather different setting.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:32.900
Ok, so the plot gets a bit convoluted but
then so do the plots of many traditional spy

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:33.900
movies.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:39.580
So, to sum up: Spy delivers just what you
want from a comedy: lots of big belly laughs

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.460
and great performances.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.090
Spy gets 4 out of 5 Flicks.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:46.419
Now, what do you think of Spy?

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:47.960
And what are your favourite action-comedies?

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:53.050
Oh, are you looking forward to Spy director
Paul Feig's Ghostbusters movie?

